# 01 - The People We Let Inside
![01 - The People we let inside]60f472fd-2cfc-09fd-b4fd-0f7f9696919c.jpeg
by Obinna D. Ugoeke, Snr

We do not fear strangers. We fear the people we let inside.

Strangers cannot hurt us the way familiar people can. A stranger does not know where we keep our soft parts. A stranger does not know what we cry about when nobody is watching. A stranger does not know the exact word that will break us.

But the people we let inside know.

We gave them the key. We showed them the rooms. We told them, "This is where I am fragile. Do not touch here."

And most times, they do not touch it on purpose. They just forget. They get careless. They get comfortable. They start to treat your heart like furniture — something that will always be there, no matter how they handle it.

That is how it happens. Not with a big betrayal. With small carelessness.

A missed call that was once an emergency. A reply that used to come in seconds, now comes in days. A joke about the thing you once whispered in confidence.

You watch it and you say nothing. Because you do not want to look weak. Because you do not want to lose them. So you shrink. You learn to need less. You learn to laugh at things that hurt.

Until one day you realize: you are lonelier inside this relationship than you were when you were alone.

And that is the saddest kind of loneliness. The one that happens with people around you.

So you start to lock doors again. Quietly. One by one. Not out of hate. Out of survival.

They will say you changed. They will say you became cold.

They will never say: "We are the reason he had to protect himself from us."

Because the people we let inside rarely understand what it cost us to let them in.

